 
          <p>As part of our yearly month-before-Christmas tradition, we re-watch early nineties local production TV show <em>Dag Sinterklaas</em>. I tried <a href="/post/2025/12/sinterklaas-likes-playing-on-the-game-boy/">explaining the tradition last year</a> in case you&rsquo;re interested in weird Flemish/Dutch culture. When Sinterklaas returns to Spain on the 6th of December, all little kids&mdash;correction, little kids who have been good&mdash;are gifted something to celebrate the <em>Goedheiligen man</em> (&ldquo;good holy man&rdquo; in English I guess?). Since there are 2x10 episodes of <em>Dag Sinterklaas</em>, last Monday marked the start of the tradition.</p>
<p>In episode 3, <em>Het boek van Sinterklaas</em> (the book of Sinterklaas), Bart and Zwarte Piet are tasked with finding the book, glasses, and pen of a distracted Sint that claims Piet misplaced these items while in fact he did so himself. The horse <em>Slecht Weer Vandaag</em> (Bad Weather Today&mdash;yes, that&rsquo;s the name of the horse, long story) tipped off the location of the book: inside the fridge. Bart almost breaks the Sint&rsquo;s glasses while sitting on his bed.</p>
<p>But it is the pen that interested me the most. Piet finds it on a shelf in the bathroom next to the shampoo.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/sinterklaas-writes-with-a-montblanc/piet.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/sinterklaas-writes-with-a-montblanc/piet.jpg" loading="lazy" title="Piet finds the Sint&#39;s expensive pen in the bathroom." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>Piet finds the Sint&#39;s expensive pen in the bathroom.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Zijn pen! Zijn dure pen! Ik, slordig? Ha! (His pen! His expensive pen! Me, carless? Ha!)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the next scene we see the duo returning to Sinterklaas where he explains what the book is about and how he writes in it. During the explanation he waves about with the black expensive looking pen with golden nib where, if you look closely, you&rsquo;ll spot a distinct white mark on top of the cap. That&rsquo;s right: the signature Montblanc mark.</p>
<p>That mean only one thing: the Flemish 1992 version of Sinterklaas writes with a Montblanc.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/sinterklaas-writes-with-a-montblanc/pen.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/sinterklaas-writes-with-a-montblanc/pen.jpg" loading="lazy" title="The Sint in denial: I didn&#39;t put those things there!" data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>The Sint in denial: I didn&#39;t put those things there!</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>Wait. Back up a minute. I recently read an article about world leaders and their signature pens. When it comes to holy men, I&rsquo;m certain Sinterklaas should be added to that list of leaders on the spiritual (Christian) side. Aqeeb wrote in <a href="https://aqeebbb.substack.com/p/the-pens-that-run-the-world">The Pens That Rule The World</a> (sadly a Substack link) that King Charles III tried to write a guestbook with a leaky Montblanc. The result was recorded: &ldquo;I can’t bear this bloody thing, every stinking time&rdquo;. Ouch.</p>
<p>But that was a special sterling silver edition. Montblanc&rsquo;s flagship pen, the Meisterstück 149&mdash;the one I suspect is also the one Piet found in the Sint&rsquo;s bathroom&mdash;is, according to Aqeeb&rsquo;s article, &ldquo;the pen chancellors and popes reach for. Konrad Adenauer used one. Helmut Kohl used one. A pope used one to sign his own resignation.&rdquo;. Yet it is not just the popes:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The 149 is the most common pen (highly duped/faked too) of its kind there is, the one a junior diplomat buys at duty-free before his first summit so he looks the part. The most powerful and the most nervous reach for the same pen.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Perhaps the one Sinterklaas misplaced is a fake one. In another episode, Piet tries to trick Sint into drinking ink instead of wine. Perhaps they poured wine into the converter. Better to do this with a fake one; who knows what will happen with an expensive nib like that.</p>
<p>The true pen afflictionado would no doubt ask: what nib size? A quick glance at the big book reveals a huge gothic-like handwriting. That can only mean one thing: a triple broad-esque <em>signature</em> nib, Montblanc&rsquo;s specialty that no sane man can and will write with.</p>
<p>When it comes to ink, the Sint is quite traditional: manuscript-like brown. Which is weird considering the future episode that displays pitch blank ink.</p>
<p>Or perhaps he writes with molten Sint/Piet chocolate figures as ink. In that case I&rsquo;d pour that ink down my throat as well.</p>


          
